Purpose: Perform ensemble docking, incorporating some degree of dynamic
  pocket-side behavior such as induced fit. Proteins must first be properly
  structure-repaired with tools such as pdbfixer.

Requirements:
  Basic policy:
    - Set this up as a new project for a general-purpose tool (a reusable
      package usable for any target, not tied to a specific target/ligand
      set; same policy as rocslib/omegalib/plviewerlib)
    - Working directory is ~/work/docking; consolidate modules under
      dockinglib, splitting reusable functions/classes as much as possible
    - Use the conda env `mpro` (vina, meeko, pdbfixer, openmm,
      openmmforcefields, openff-toolkit, mdtraj already present). No need to
      create a new env
    - CLI uses argparse. Split into three console commands, dockinglib-prep /
      dockinglib-dock / dockinglib-refine (so receptor-ensemble preparation /
      screening / post-MD processing can be run independently, while also
      allowing an end-to-end run via pipeline.py)
    - For long-running batch processing, print one line of progress per
      completed item with print(flush=True) (the rocslib/progress.py
      approach, not a tqdm-style progress bar)
    - Independent tasks (e.g. ligand x ensemble-member combinations) should be
      runnable via a ProcessPoolExecutor-based parallel map (--n-jobs,
      sequential by default)

  Ensemble structure (multiple receptor conformations):
    - Take multiple PDBs (co-crystal structures) as input. Each structure is
      individually structure-repaired with PDBFixer (filling in missing
      residues/atoms, replacing nonstandard residues, removing heteroatoms
      such as water/ions/co-crystallized ligands). No hydrogens are added at
      this stage (since the downstream Meeko performs its own protonation/
      charge assignment, and adding hydrogens beforehand would conflict with
      that)
    - After chain extraction on each structure, renumber residues 1..N,
      insert TER at backbone breaks, and rename disulfide-bonded CYS to CYX
      (to avoid problems in PDBFixer/Meeko; auto-detected by distance)
    - Determine the docking box (center, size, with specifiable padding) from
      each member's co-crystallized ligand coordinates

  Pocket-side dynamic behavior (induced fit) is represented in two stages:
    1. Vina + Meeko flexible side-chain docking: automatically detect pocket
       residues within a distance cutoff (default 5 Å, specifiable) from the
       co-crystallized ligand, and generate rigid/flex PDBQT pairs using
       Meeko's `mk_prepare_receptor.py -f FLEXRES`. Vina also accepts both
       rigid_pdbqt and flex_pdbqt (using the Python API
       `Vina.set_receptor(rigid, flex)`)
    2. Short local MD post-processing with OpenMM, applied only to top-ranked
       poses: using a realistic CPU setup with implicit solvent (GBn2) +
       hydrogen mass repartitioning (4 fs), run minimization → equilibration →
       production, then judge stability from the ligand heavy-atom RMSD
       time series and re-rank (limited to the top N hits rather than all, to
       keep computational cost down)

  Screening (the docking itself):
    - Ligand input is `.smi` (SMILES + ID, tab/space separated)
    - SMILES → 3D embedding (ETKDGv3) → MMFF optimization → ligand PDBQT
      generation via Meeko
    - Dock each ligand against every member of the ensemble, keeping the
      per-member affinity while ranking ligands by their best (lowest)
      affinity (best-of-ensemble approach)
    - Output results as a CSV (rank, ligand ID, SMILES, best member, best
      affinity, per-member affinities, pose PDBQT path for downstream MD, and
      receptor PDB path) plus pose SDFs for the top hits
    - Pose SDFs use the property name `affinity` for the score so they can be
      loaded directly into the existing protein-ligand viewer
      (~/work/viewer, plviewerlib) (one of
      plviewerlib.scoring.SCORE_PROPERTY_CANDIDATES)

  Test data:
    - Multiple SARS-CoV-2 Mpro PDB structures with genuinely different pocket
      conformations (e.g. 6W63 / 7L11 / 7L10, each bound to a different
      inhibitor), fetched from RCSB, used as ensemble members
    - A `.smi` library containing each member's co-crystallized ligand (for
      self-docking validity checks) mixed with a few unrelated molecules such
      as approved drugs

  Validation:
    - The generated rigid/flex PDBQTs load into Vina and grid maps can be
      computed
    - Per-member affinities actually vary (evidence that the ensemble is
      working)
    - MD post-processing completes in a realistic time on CPU, and the
      RMSD/stability columns are populated
    - The output receptor PDBs and pose SDFs can be loaded by the existing
      plviewerlib viewer
    - pytest smoke tests (tests/, in the rocslib/tests/ style; pure-Python
      parts only: pocket-residue detection, flexres formatting, ranking
      logic, CSV I/O)

  Documentation:
    - Provide PROMPT (this file) and README.md (setup, usage, CLI examples,
      module layout)
